# tests/assertions/end_window.rxt — [OPT-ENDWIN], `[OPTLOOP.1]` batch 1
# (D119): THE END-ANCHOR START WINDOW'S correctness carve-outs.
#
# WHAT THE MECHANISM DOES, so these cells are readable as a net rather than
# as a pile: when every alternative of a pattern ends in `$`/`\Z`/`\z`
# outside multiline AND its maximum width is finite, both engines' search
# entries RAISE `search_from` to `subject_length - (maxw + eps)` before any
# attempt runs — `eps` is 1 for `$`/`\Z` (they hold before a final newline)
# and 0 for `\z`. `docs/spec/tuning.md` §2.26, `src/opt/endwin.c`.
#
# WHY IT NEEDS A CORPUS FILE WHEN ITS TWO BATCH SIBLINGS DO NOT. [OPT-ANCHOR-VM]
# and [OPT-REQBYTE] remove only work an artifact would have done and thrown
# away, so no answer can move and their only detector is a stamp. THIS one
# moves the position a search STARTS AT, and a window one byte too narrow
# DELETES A LEGAL MATCH — silently, and only on subjects longer than the
# window. Every block below is a cell where a plausible off-by-one or a
# plausible over-generalisation of the view test produces a different answer.
#
# THE SUBJECTS ARE LONG ON PURPOSE. A window is only consulted when
# `subject_length > maxw + eps`; a 3-byte subject exercises the guard's
# false arm and nothing else. Each block therefore carries a SHORT subject
# (the mechanism inert) and a LONG one (the mechanism firing) for the same
# claim, so a regression cannot hide in the half nobody wrote.
#
# ORACLE. `$`, `\z`, `(?m)$` and `\b` blocks are python3 `re`-verifiable and
# are verified by tests/harness/verify_rxt.py like any base-tier cell
# (python's `\Z` IS PCRE2's `\z`, which is the only mapping this file needs).
# The `\Z` blocks carry `# pcre2-only` and are verified by
# tests/assertions/verify_pcre2.py instead — `absolute.rxt`'s own header
# carries that rule and the measured divergence behind it, and it applies
# here for the same reason whether or not these particular subjects expose it.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE CANNOT SEE: it pins ANSWERS. That the window was DERIVED at
# all, that a declining pattern emitted no clamp, and that the stamp agrees
# with the emitted bound are tests/codegen/run_prechecks.sh §2's job.


# ======================================================================
# (1) `$`: the FINAL-NEWLINE ALLOWANCE is the whole of `eps`
#
# `abc$` has maxw 3 and eps 1, so the window is 4 and a search over a long
# subject begins at `n - 4`. On "...abc\n" the match starts at `n - 4`
# EXACTLY — one byte narrower and this block is a lost match, which is the
# direction the sabotage row plants.
# ======================================================================

pattern abc$
features assertions
m "abc" 0 3
m "abc\n" 0 3
n "abcx"
n "abc\nx"
n "abc\n\n"
m "xxxabc" 3 6
m "xxxabc\n" 3 6
m "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxabc" 32 35
m "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxabc\n" 32 35
n "abcxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx"
n "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx"

# The same pattern asked from a startpos INSIDE the window and from one
# BEYOND the match's start: the clamp may only ever RAISE `search_from`, so
# a caller who asked for a later position must still get the later answer.
pattern abc$
features assertions
ns 1 "abc"
ms 0 "xxxabc" 3 6
ms 3 "xxxabc" 3 6
ns 4 "xxxabc"


# ======================================================================
# (2) `\z` AGAINST `$` ON THE SAME SUBJECT — the `eps` distinction, which is
# a DIFFERENT WINDOW WIDTH and not only a different accept test. `abc\z` has
# window 3; `abc$` has window 4. The trailing-newline subject is where the
# two answers separate, and it separates them at BOTH lengths.
# ======================================================================

pattern abc\z
features assertions
m "abc" 0 3
n "abc\n"
m "xxxabc" 3 6
n "xxxabc\n"
m "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxabc" 32 35
n "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxabc\n"


# ======================================================================
# (3) `\Z` IS `$`'s EXACT ALIAS HERE, which is why the analysis gives them
# one arm. If a later change made `\Z` take `\z`'s window, the
# trailing-newline rows below become lost matches.
# ======================================================================

# pcre2-only
pattern abc\Z
features assertions
m "abc" 0 3
m "abc\n" 0 3
n "abc\nx"
m "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxabc" 32 35
m "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxabc\n" 32 35


# ======================================================================
# (4) MULTILINE DECLINES — D62 control 3. Under `(?m)` a `$` holds before
# EVERY newline, so no window exists at all. A clamp emitted here would
# lose every match but the last line's, which is what these rows say.
# ======================================================================

pattern (?m)abc$
features assertions,modifiers
m "abc\ndef" 0 3
m "xxabc\nyy" 2 5
m "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxabc\nyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyy" 32 35
m "abc\nxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx" 0 3


# ======================================================================
# (5) AN UNBOUNDED WIDTH DECLINES. `a+$` is end-anchored and has no finite
# maximum, so there is no window to compute. The long row is a match whose
# start is arbitrarily far from the end.
# ======================================================================

pattern a+$
features assertions
m "aaa" 0 3
m "xaaa" 1 4
m "xaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa" 1 36


# ======================================================================
# (6) A BOUNDED QUANTIFIER IS THE WINDOW'S ARITHMETIC. `a{0,4}$` has maxw 4
# and window 5; the nullable body makes the EMPTY match at the subject end
# the leftmost answer on a subject with no trailing `a`, which a window that
# started one byte too late would miss entirely.
# ======================================================================

pattern a{0,4}$
features assertions
m "" 0 0
m "xxxxx" 5 5
m "xxaaa" 2 5
m "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx" 32 32
m "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxaaaa" 28 32


# ======================================================================
# (7) THE WIDEST ALTERNATIVE SETS THE WINDOW, and the NARROWEST match must
# still be found. `(?:foo|barbaz)$` has window 7; a `foo` match starts 3
# bytes from the end, comfortably inside it, and a window computed from the
# FIRST branch rather than the widest would still find this one — so the
# `barbaz` row is the one that discriminates.
# ======================================================================

pattern (?:foo|barbaz)$
features assertions
m "xxfoo" 2 5
m "barbaz" 0 6
n "fooo"
m "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxfoo" 32 35
m "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxbarbaz" 30 36


# ======================================================================
# (8) A ZERO-WIDTH PATTERN: window 0 for `\z`, 1 for `$`. The empty match at
# the subject end is the only answer, and `$`'s own row additionally has to
# find it BEFORE the final newline.
# ======================================================================

pattern \z
features assertions
m "" 0 0
m "abc" 3 3
ms 1 "abc" 3 3
m "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx" 32 32

pattern $
features assertions
m "" 0 0
m "abc" 3 3
m "abc\n" 3 3
m "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx" 32 32
m "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx\n" 31 31


# ======================================================================
# (9) A WORD-CONTEXT SEED AT THE CLAMPED POSITION. `\bfoo$` starts the scan
# at `n - 4`, where the machine's start state depends on `subject[n - 5]` —
# the byte BEFORE the clamped position. A clamp that moved the position
# without letting the seed read its own context byte answers these rows
# wrong in both directions.
# ======================================================================

pattern \bfoo$
features assertions
m "a foo" 2 5
n "afoo"
m "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx foo" 32 35
n "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxfoo"


# ======================================================================
# (10) ANCHORED AT BOTH ENDS — the [OPT-ANCHOR-VM] interaction. `^abc$`
# carries a start bound of 0 AND a window of 4, and on a long subject the
# two ranges are DISJOINT. The correct answer is no match, reached by
# arithmetic rather than by a rule about their interaction, and the short
# rows are what say the composition did not break the ordinary case.
# ======================================================================

pattern ^abc$
features assertions
m "abc" 0 3
m "abc\n" 0 3
n "xabc"
n "abcx"
n "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxabc"
n "abcxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx"


# ======================================================================
# (11) CAPTURES, so the VM route's own copy of the clamp is exercised on a
# long subject. A capturing group forces the VM, where the clamp sits at the
# top of `<prefix>_search_run` rather than in the DFA's search entry.
# ======================================================================

pattern (ab)(c)$
features assertions
m "abc" 0 3
m "xxabc" 2 5
m "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxabc" 30 33
n "abcxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx"
